module domicile.mojom;

import "third_party/blink/public/mojom/tokens/tokens.mojom";
import "url/mojom/url.mojom";

// A <webview> asking the browser for a guest to put behind it.
//
// WHY THE ELEMENT HAS TO ASK. Patch 0007 makes <webview> report
// FrameOwnerElementType::kIframe on purpose, so that the fork stays out of
// every switch on that enum -- which leaves the browser process unable to tell
// a <webview> from an <iframe> by looking. Nothing in the frame tree carries
// the signal, so the element carries it, over this. Keying on an explicit
// request is also what extensions' <webview> does.
//
// WHAT A GUEST BUYS, and why it is not a carve-out in AncestorThrottle: a
// guest's main frame IS a main frame, so it has no ancestor for
// X-Frame-Options or CSP frame-ancestors to be checked against, history is the
// guest's own across processes, and its storage is first-party rather than
// partitioned as a third party. See docs/architecture/
// BROWSER-WINDOW-PARITY.md in the Domicile repository.
//
// THE ACCESS CONTROL IS THE BINDER'S, not this file's, and it is the same one
// ControlChannel has: the browser binds WebViewGuestHost only for a document
// whose origin is the shell's. domicile:// is neither web-safe nor
// CORS-enabled, so no ordinary web content can hold that origin. A shell that
// asks for a guest can already ask for `Spawn`, so there is nothing here to
// protect it from itself.

// Renderer -> browser, bound per document.
interface WebViewGuestHost {
  // Give `placeholder_frame` a guest page.
  //
  // `placeholder_frame` is the child frame the <webview>'s frame owner already
  // created -- the one an <iframe> would navigate. It is what
  // WebContents::AttachInnerWebContents wants, and it is left on about:blank
  // because attaching swaps it out and a frame with a beforeunload handler in
  // it would have to be asked first.
  //
  // The browser refuses a token that is not a child of the asking document's
  // own frame: the element may claim a guest for its own placeholder and for
  // nothing else.
  //
  // Nothing comes back. Attaching is asynchronous -- the frame has to be
  // prepared first -- and the element has nothing to do differently either
  // way; what it does next is Navigate, which is safe before the attach
  // finishes and is why this is not a callback.
  CreateGuest(blink.mojom.LocalFrameToken placeholder_frame,
              pending_receiver<WebViewGuest> guest);
};

// One guest, for as long as the element that asked for it lives.
//
// A pipe of its own rather than more methods on WebViewGuestHost taking a
// frame token: attaching DESTROYS the placeholder frame, so the token that
// named the guest stops naming anything the moment the guest exists, and a
// second src would have nothing to send.
interface WebViewGuest {
  // Load `url` in the guest, as a top-level navigation of the guest's own
  // page. This is what `src` does, and what it does instead of navigating the
  // frame the element owns.
  Navigate(url.mojom.Url url);
};
